<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Gabali</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.) · capital Anderitum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountain people of the Lozère, clients of the Arverni, who gave their name to the Gévaudan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Gabali lived on the granite plateaus and valleys of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a>, in the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozere">Lozère</a>. Caesar names them as clients of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny</a> praises the cheese of their district, which was sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. No rulers or institutions are recorded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They took part in the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> in 52 BCE, attacking the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvii">Helvii</a> on the border of the Province. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> their chief town was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javols">Anderitum</a> (Javols), and their name lived on in the medieval county and bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gevaudan">Gévaudan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:53.73%;width:4.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl 